Create vocabulary data for the supplied {source_language} words or expressions.

Treat bracketed input as context for identifying the sense used there, but do
not limit the result to that contextual sense. For each supplied term, identify
every genuinely disjoint, commonly used lexical sense that a learner is
reasonably likely to encounter in the relevant language, period, and register.
Create a fully separate JSON card entry for each such sense, resulting in
separate cards rather than combining multiple senses in one entry. Do this even
when the supplied context makes one particular sense unambiguous. Return the
contextual sense first, followed by the other common disjoint senses.

Treat senses as disjoint only when the term expresses a genuinely different
meaning, not merely a different connotation, degree, register, implication,
typical context, or other nuance of the same underlying meaning. Do not create
separate entries for rare, obsolete outside the selected historical register,
or highly technical senses unless they are independently useful to the
learner. All requested fields in one card entry must describe only that entry's
single sense. Skip an entry that does not exist and silently correct an obvious
typo.

Return only the fields required by the supplied response schema. Never invent
extra fields and never return null in place of requested text.
